# safe-route-ai

Improved road accident hotspot prediction app built with Python, traditional ML, map visualization, and transparent evaluation.

This project keeps the original idea of the Road Accident Hotspot Prediction project but makes the implementation more realistic and safer to reuse.

Important note
- The original project is by madhan112007.
- The upstream repository does not currently show a visible OSS license in the repository metadata.
- The improved version here preserves attribution and keeps the model and explanations clearly separated.

What this project does
- Trains a traditional machine learning model to identify high-risk accident locations based on historical patterns.
- Clusters accident locations into hotspot groups using geospatial clustering.
- Shows hotspots on a map.
- Uses a small LLM explanation layer only to explain the model result in simple language.
- Reports proper evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC) and includes a limitations section.
- Does not claim real-time accident prediction unless the feature is implemented and validated.

Project structure
- app.py — main Streamlit application
- config.py — project configuration and dataset settings
- src/
  - data_loader.py — downloads and prepares the accident dataset
  - modeling.py — train/evaluate the Random Forest model
  - hotspots.py — cluster accident points into hotspot groups
  - explainer.py — simple LLM explanation layer with rule-based fallback
- requirements.txt — project dependencies

Dataset source
- The app downloads the original project dataset from the upstream repository when it is not already present.
- This is used for historical risk modeling and hotspot analysis only.

Installation

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run app.py
```

Environment variables
- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — optional, for the small LLM explanation feature
- `OPENWEATHER_KEY` — optional future enhancement

Limitations
- This is a historical-risk model, not live accident detection.
- Data may reflect reporting bias and incomplete coverage.
- The app is intended for educational and research-oriented exploration.
- Spatial clustering and model predictions should be treated as decision support, not safety-critical automation.

